course: euphemisms
lesson: 18
title: Soft words for hard things: animals and pets and money and poverty
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: People use soft words for things about animals they find unpleasant: toilets, killing and giving pets away.
explain: Farmers, hunters and officials often use dry words like "culled", "processed" or "pest control" so killing sounds like ordinary work.
explain: Owners say a pet was "rehomed" or is "free to a good home" so giving it away sounds kind rather than unwanted.
explain: Money words are soft too: an official says "low-income" instead of "poor", and a friend says "money's a bit tight" instead of "I'm broke".
explain: For example, a cat that "left a present" on the doormat may have brought in a dead mouse.
fact: "Altered" (US) means a pet has been neutered or spayed.
fact: "Doctored" (old-fashioned US) means a pet has been neutered.
fact: "Do its business" means a pet goes to the toilet.
fact: "Made a mess" means a pet went to the toilet indoors.
fact: "Left a present" is a joking way to say a pet left droppings or a dead animal.
fact: "Culled" means animals were killed to reduce their numbers.
fact: "Harvested" (US hunting jargon) means an animal was killed by hunters.
fact: "Processed" means farm animals were slaughtered for meat.
fact: "Dispatched" means an animal was killed quickly by a hunter or farmer.
fact: "Rehomed" means a pet was given away to new owners.
fact: "Surrendered" means a pet was given up to an animal shelter.
fact: "Free to a good home" means the owner no longer wants the animal and is giving it away.
fact: "Sent to the knacker's yard" (old-fashioned UK) means an old horse was sent to be slaughtered.
fact: "Went to the glue factory" is a joking way to say an old horse was slaughtered.
fact: "Pest control" means killing unwanted animals such as rats or mice.
fact: "Economically disadvantaged" (official) means poor.
fact: "Underprivileged" means poor and without the advantages most people have.
fact: "Low-income" (official) means poor.
fact: "Financially challenged" is a joking way to say poor or broke.
fact: "Money's a bit tight" means I do not have enough money.
exercise: A US shelter lists a cat as "altered". What does it mean? | The cat is neutered or spayed | The cat's name was changed | The cat had its fur cut | The cat changed colour
exercise: An old farmer says his tomcat was "doctored". What happened? | He was neutered | He was treated for a cold | He became a vet | He was given a check-up
exercise: "Take the dog out to do its business." What should the dog do? | Go to the toilet | Fetch the newspaper | Earn some money | Do a trick for visitors
exercise: "The puppy made a mess on the carpet." What did the puppy do? | Went to the toilet indoors | Tore up the carpet | Spilled its water | Dragged mud in from outside
exercise: "The cat left me a present on the mat." What is probably on the mat? | Droppings or a dead animal | A wrapped gift with a bow on top | A shiny toy | A birthday card
exercise: The news says hundreds of deer were "culled". What happened? | They were killed to reduce their numbers | They were counted one by one from a helicopter | They were moved to a new forest | They were fed in winter
exercise: A US hunter says he "harvested" a deer. What did he do? | He killed it | He picked fruit near it | He photographed it | He caught it alive
exercise: At a meat plant, chickens are "processed". What does this mean? | They are slaughtered for meat | They are sorted by size | They are given vaccines | They are packed alive for sale
exercise: The farmer "dispatched" the injured rabbit. What did he do? | Killed it quickly | Posted it to a vet | Sent it to another farm | Let it go
exercise: The family "rehomed" their dog. What happened? | They gave it away to new owners | They built it a new kennel | They moved house with it | They took it home from the vet
exercise: A pet was "surrendered" to the shelter. What does it mean? | The owner gave it up to the shelter | The pet gave up fighting | The pet was caught by police | The shelter gave it back
exercise: A sign says "Kittens free to a good home". What is really going on? | The owner is giving them away and does not want them | The kittens are looking for a house to buy | The kittens are being sold cheaply | The home comes free with the kittens
exercise: In old Britain, a horse "sent to the knacker's yard" went where? | To be slaughtered | To a riding school | To a horse show | To a field to rest
exercise: "That old nag went to the glue factory." What happened to the horse? | It was slaughtered | It got a job in a factory | It was stuck in glue | It retired to a farm
exercise: The council called in "pest control" for the rats. What will happen? | The rats will be killed | The rats will be counted | The rats will be moved to a park | The rats will be fed
exercise: A report talks about "economically disadvantaged" families. What are they? | Poor | Bad at economics | Abroad | Owning a business
exercise: What does "underprivileged children" mean? | Poor children without the usual advantages | Children who misbehave in class and at home | Children with no brothers or sisters | Children under ten
exercise: A form asks if you are from a "low-income" family. What does it mean? | Poor | Living in a low house | Small | Paying little rent on purpose
exercise: Uncle jokes that he is "financially challenged". What does he mean? | He is broke | He is good at money puzzles | He works in a bank | He lost a maths contest
exercise: Mum says, "Money's a bit tight this month." What does she mean? | We do not have enough money | Her purse is too small | Coins are stuck together | We have lots of money saved
test: "omg the cat left a present in my slipper" — what did the cat probably leave? | Droppings or a dead mouse | A toy mouse it found under the bed | A ribbon | Some cat food
test: A news line: "Badgers to be culled in three counties to stop cattle disease." What will happen to the badgers? | Many will be killed | They will get medicine | They will be counted | They will be moved to zoos
test: Ad: "Lovely rabbit, free to a good home, ASAP." ("ASAP" means as soon as possible.) What does "ASAP" tell you about the owner? | They want to get rid of it quickly | They will charge a lot later | They will keep it for ever | They are giving away their house
test: "dog's made a mess again, bloody hell, who left the door shut" — what has the dog done? | Gone to the toilet indoors | Knocked over a vase of flowers | Chewed a shoe | Spilled its water bowl
test: Jess says a "harvested" deer means it was picked like an apple. Right? | No, it means it was killed by a hunter | Yes, deer grow on farms | Yes, it means it was fed | No, it means it was photographed
test: An advert says: "Our chickens are humanely processed." What happens to the chickens? | They are slaughtered for meat | They are washed and brushed | They are taught tricks | They are sent to farms
test: "We can't do the trip, money's a bit tight, sorry love." Why is there no trip? | They do not have enough money | The trip is far too far away to drive | The car is too small | Love is busy
test: A charity says it helps "underprivileged kids" go to summer camp. Which children? | Poor children | Children who are naughty | Children who are very clever | Children with no pets
test: Grandad: "In my day, an old horse that couldn't work went to the knacker's yard." What happened to such horses? | They were slaughtered | They were given a rest | They were sold to a circus | They were fed better
test: "Dad says Max was rehomed, so he's alive right?" — is Max alive? | Yes, he was given to new owners | No, he died | No, he was put down | Yes, he is still in the house with Dad
test: The farmer had to "dispatch" a sheep hurt by a car. What did he do? | Killed it quickly | Posted it to the vet | Sent it to market | Took it to hospital
test: A man jokes, "I'm not poor, I'm financially challenged!" What is he? | Poor | Rich | Good with money | A banker
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a domestic animal described as having been "surrendered" to a shelter? | The owner relinquished the animal to the shelter | The animal ceased resisting capture | The animal was apprehended by authorities | The shelter returned the animal to its owner
exam: It is given that a quantity of wild deer has been "culled" pursuant to a management plan. Identify the principal meaning of the term. | The animals were killed to reduce their number | The animals were relocated to a reserve | The animals were enumerated | The animals were provided with winter feed
exam: Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the designation "economically disadvantaged" as applied to a household. | The household is poor | The household lacks knowledge of economics | The household resides overseas | The household operates a commercial enterprise
exam: Whereas a local authority engages "pest control" in respect of a rodent infestation, determine the action thereby undertaken. | The rodents are killed | The rodents are enumerated | The rodents are relocated to parkland | The rodents are provided with food
